[2025-04-30 13:17:26] PROPOSAL by USA:
Title: Untitled Proposal
### Title: A Pragmatic Framework for Climate Action: Flexible Global Carbon Pricing and Innovation Support  

#### Preamble:  
Acknowledging the urgent threat of climate change and the need for collective global action, this proposal aims to bridge the divergence of perspectives by promoting a flexible, equitable, and innovation-driven approach to reducing carbon emissions. It respects the principles of equity and common but differentiated responsibilities, while fostering economic growth and technological innovation across both developed and developing nations.  

#### Proposal:  
1. **Adopt a Flexible Carbon Pricing Framework**:  
   Nations will have the option to implement carbon pricing mechanisms, either through a carbon tax, cap-and-trade systems, or a combination thereof, tailored to their unique economic circumstances and development priorities. Developed nations will aim for higher carbon pricing as a demonstration of leadership, while developing economies will be granted longer timelines and greater flexibility to implement mechanisms in line with their developmental goals.  

2. **Establish a Green Innovation Fund**:  
   Create an international fund supported by contributions from high-income nations and private sector partners. This fund will provide financial and technical assistance to developing countries for deploying renewable energy, enhancing energy efficiency, and advancing climate-resilient infrastructure. It will also support the global transfer of green technologies to ensure that innovation is accessible to all nations.  

3. **Revenue Allocation for Equity and Resilience**:  
   A portion of revenues generated from national carbon pricing mechanisms will be directed toward climate adaptation and mitigation efforts in vulnerable nations. This promotes fairness by enabling smaller or developing states to address climate impacts while building resilience.  

This framework represents a balanced, politically viable path forward that encourages climate action, respects national sovereignty, and promotes economic growth through innovation. Nations are urged to embrace this policy to advance shared climate goals while fostering global equity and prosperity.  

#### Call to Action:  
The United States urges immediate adoption of this proposal and welcomes constructive collaboration to further refine its implementation.  

---  


[2025-04-30 13:17:32] PROPOSAL by China:
Title: **Title:**
Balanced Framework for Global Climate Action: Flexible Carbon Pricing and Support Mechanisms

**Proposal:**  
Recognizing the urgent threat posed by climate change and the diverse circumstances of member nations, we propose the following balanced approach to global carbon policy:

**Action Items:**

1. **Flexible National Carbon Pricing:**  
Rather than imposing a uniform global carbon tax, all countries shall be required to implement carbon pricing systems or equivalent climate policies tailored to their national contexts and stages of development. Developed nations are encouraged to adopt stronger, binding carbon pricing mechanisms immediately, while developing nations may set more gradual timelines and approaches that safeguard economic growth and poverty alleviation.

2. **Enhanced Climate Finance and Technology Transfer:**  
Developed nations shall commit to significantly increasing financial and technological support for developing countries, facilitating their transition to low-carbon economies. A portion of any revenue generated by national carbon pricing systems may be contributed to an international fund dedicated to technology transfer, capacity building, and adaptation measures in developing nations.

3. **Transparent Reporting and Cooperative Review:**  
All nations will submit biennial progress reports on their carbon pricing implementation or equivalent measures. An international committee shall facilitate transparent review and constructive dialogue, respecting national circumstances and aiming to strengthen collective ambition without imposing punitive measures on developing nations.

**Rationale:**  
This framework upholds the principle of common but differentiated responsibilities, encourages ambitious climate action, and recognizes the need for equity and economic development. It offers a pragmatic path for durable consensus and meaningful progress.

**We urge all delegates to support this proposal for fair, effective, and inclusive climate action.**

